<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Yuan</h1><p class="st-meta">1368 CE – 1635 CE · capital Karakorum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Mongol dynasty that continued on the steppe after being driven out of China in 1368, outlasting the Ming's attempts to destroy it for nearly three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1368 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> armies approached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toghon-temur">Toghon Temür</a>, last emperor of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>, fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Shangdu</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yingchang">Yingchang</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayushiridara">Ayushiridara</a> re-established the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>, still claiming rule over all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and his generals defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolia">Mongolia</a> in 1372.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans ruled a shifting federation of Mongol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumens</a> rather than a centralised state. After a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buyur-lake">Lake Buir</a> in 1388, power fragmented, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/four-oirat">Four Oirat</a> often dominated; their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esen-taishi">Esen</a> captured the Ming emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumu-crisis">Tumu</a> in 1449. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a> reunited the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> around 1500 into six tumens, and from the 1570s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> spread under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ligdan-khan">Ligdan Khan</a>, the last great khan, tried to restore central power but was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> and died in 1634. In 1635 his son Ejei surrendered with the Yuan imperial seal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-jin-1616-1636">Later Jin</a> (1616-1636), who soon founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="khanate, 1368 CE – 1635 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">khanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.93%;width:5.31%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1368 CE – 1635 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Karakorum</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ayushiridara, Dayan Khan, Ligdan Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism, Tibetan Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Mongol</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, <a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Northern Yuan dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1502&amp;focus=northern-yuan" aria-label="Northern Yuan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7497 671 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Northern Yuan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7817 927L7867 1005M7817 927L8018 907M7817 927L7768 1401"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7867 1005h0M8018 907h0M7768 1401h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7817 927h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7817 927h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1502&amp;focus=northern-yuan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
